<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinti</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sinti are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani people</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, settled in German-speaking lands since the 15th century. Persecuted by the Nazi regime as racially alien, together with the Roma they were deported to camps and murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> known as the Porajmos, in which hundreds of thousands of Sinti and Roma died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> officially recognised their persecution as genocide in 1982.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sinti.jpg" alt="Sinti" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J0525-0500-003,_Rheinland,_Sinti_und_Roma_mit_Wohnwagen_auf_Landstra%C3%9Fe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…by the Nuremberg Laws of September 1935 and terrorised in the November pogrom of 1938, after which their businesses were confiscated; Roma and <b>Sinti</b>, homosexual men and people labelled ‘asocial’ were also persecuted, and about 70,000 disabled patients were murdered in the Aktion T4 programme by August 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carinthian Slovenes and the Croats and Hungarians of Burgenland received minority rights under the State Treaty of 1955, and the Roma and <b>Sinti</b> were recognised in 1994.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Axis Powers in Europe led by Nazi Germany took part in the extermination of millions of Jews, Poles, Roma, <b>Sinti</b> and others in the Holocaust.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
